WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.295 --> 00:00:03.830
- [Narrator] For the past several years,

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:06.550
SpaceX has been developing Starship,

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:08.870
a giant, new deep space rocket

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:11.383
at the company's test
facility in South Texas.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.510
- [Man] Starship heading
back to the landing zone.

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:16.690
- [Narrator] Testing
of Starship prototypes

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:18.783
has resulted in fiery failures.

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:24.050
But last year, Starship
completed its first successful

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:26.620
sub-orbital flight and landing.

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:29.590
Now, SpaceX CEO, Elon Musk,

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:32.180
says 2022 is the year

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:36.510
Starship will face its biggest
test yet, orbital flight.

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:38.750
- I feel, at this point, highly confident

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:40.440
that it will get to orbit this year.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.070
- [Narrator] However, it's possible

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:44.720
that Starship's historic
first orbital flight test

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:47.900
might take place at the Kennedy
Space Center in Florida,

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.800
rather than at Starbase
in Boca Chica, Texas.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:52.710
That's because flight safety regulators

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:55.343
could end up determining
more environmental reviews

00:00:55.343 --> 00:00:57.640
are needed for the Texas site.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.660
- An orbital test fight
is a really big milestone

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:01.493
for Starship,

00:01:01.493 --> 00:01:05.350
or, really, for any rocket
that's going to space.

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:09.180
- [Narrator] So why after
years of development in Texas

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:12.420
could SpaceX's Starship make
its most important flight

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:14.520
to date in Florida?

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:18.210
And what could that switch
mean for SpaceX's operations

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:19.133
going forward?

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:27.320
On Thursday, February 10th,
SpaceX CEO, Elon Musk,

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:30.570
gave his first update on
the company Starship program

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:32.023
in about two years.

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:34.420
Towering behind him

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:37.640
at the company's South
Texas Starbase was Starship.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:39.210
- The essential technology,

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:41.790
the holy grail breakthrough that's needed

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:46.790
is a rapid and completely
reusable rocket system.

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:49.320
- When stacked on top
of its massive booster

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:53.520
called Super Heavy, Starship
stands at 120 meters,

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:58.340
or 400 feet, making it the
tallest rocket ever constructed.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:03.340
- Starship, according to Elon
Musk, is meant to go to Mars.

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:05.300
Starship's rocket system is meant to be

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:10.300
a fully reusable
multi-planetary rocket system.

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:12.410
- [Narrator] NASA selected Starship

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:14.090
as the vehicle to get astronauts

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:17.450
to land on the surface
of the moon by 2025.

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:20.170
And the Pentagon said
it wants to use Starship

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:22.280
to transport military cargo.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:24.370
But before all that can happen,

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:27.140
Starship needs to be tested in orbit.

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:31.960
- SpaceX has been able to
conduct these shorter flights,

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:33.970
no more than 18 miles up.

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:37.320
But this year, Elon Musk says

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:41.670
he's pretty sure that that
Starship is gonna go to orbit.

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:44.150
- [Narrator] The orbital test
mission still needs sign off

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:46.750
from the FAA, which has been conducting

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:49.900
an environmental assessment
of the company's proposal

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.160
to operate Starship in Texas.

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:53.160
- The FAA, of course,

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.170
is even looking at this in the first place

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:59.210
because the agency monitors and regulates

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:02.580
space launch facilities,
launches themselves,

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:04.530
and then re-entries.

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:07.100
- [Narrator] Last year,
the FAA held hearings

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:10.230
about the Starship proposal
for the Texas location,

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:13.800
and received around 19,000
comments on the initiative.

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:17.670
- One of the questions with
the FAA's current review

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:21.990
for the proposal for Starship in Taxes is,

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:24.160
are they gonna have to
do additional review

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:27.010
on environmental grounds beyond that?

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:30.220
- [Narrator] In 2021, the
journal reported the Boca Chica

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:32.780
residents said the Starship
project is damaging

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:35.690
to the local wetlands and wildlife.

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:38.760
- They move in and change the whole tone

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:39.943
and tenor of the place,

00:03:39.943 --> 00:03:43.890
but they began to do 24/7 construction,

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:46.470
which meant, wall to wall,

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:51.350
people, cars, traffic,
noise, lighting, you name it.

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:53.510
- The agency is considering

00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:57.460
the potential impact of using
the vehicles at this site.

00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:59.890
Everything from noise
to what it would mean

00:03:59.890 --> 00:04:03.740
for surrounding wildlife and wetlands.

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:05.730
- [Narrator] Musk said that if in addition

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:08.720
to its current environmental
assessment, or EA,

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:13.120
the FAA requires SpaceX to
conduct a more in depth EIS,

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:15.160
or environmental impact statement,

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:18.350
of the Starbase site,
SpaceX would consider moving

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:21.580
its orbital test to the
Kennedy Space Center.

00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:23.587
In a statement, the FAA wrote,

00:04:23.587 --> 00:04:26.950
"Completing the environmental
review does not guarantee

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:30.627
that a vehicle operator license
will be issued to SpaceX."

00:04:30.627 --> 00:04:33.120
"The environmental review is just one part

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:36.480
of the FAA commercial
space licensing process.

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:40.090
SpaceX must also meet FAA safety, risk,

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:42.870
and finance responsibility requirements."

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:45.660
- It would obviously set
us back for quite some time

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:50.643
because an EIS takes a
lot longer than an EA.

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:57.547
So we would have to shift out
priorities to Cape Kennedy.

00:04:57.547 --> 00:04:59.600
- [Narrator] SpaceX already has permission

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:02.320
to use a launch site at
the Kennedy Space Center

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:03.710
for Starship.

00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:05.990
In December, Musk said in a tweet

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:09.210
that SpaceX had started
building an orbital launchpad

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:12.030
for Starship at the Florida location.

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:14.720
- The short answer to if,

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:18.390
is SpaceX abandoning Texas

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:20.560
to shift this program to Florida, is no.

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:24.530
It's possible that the
Starbase site could end up

00:05:24.530 --> 00:05:29.410
as SpaceX's kind of long term
sort of research site R&amp;D.

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:35.240
Whereas the operational sort of launchpad

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:36.460
would be in Florida.

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:39.790
They know that they're good to go there,

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:40.630
and in terms of like,

00:05:40.630 --> 00:05:43.610
all environmental reviews and analysis.

00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:45.360
It's sort of more plug and play.

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:47.560
Whereas Texas, you
still have this question

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:50.320
of what the result of the
current review is gonna be,

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:53.080
and at least the possibility
that there's gonna be

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:56.550
another environmental review beyond that.

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:58.840
- [Narrator] The FAA said
it planned to release

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:01.640
its environmental review on March 28th.

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:03.550
- We don't have a ton of insight into

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:06.060
where I think stand with the FAA.

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:10.680
We have gotten sort of a rough indication

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:14.093
that there may be an approval in March,

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:16.120
but that's all we know.

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:21.390
- [Man] We'll culminate with
landing on the landing pad

00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:22.423
in Boca Chica.

